                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6f3b951e-0169-3e93-915b-c90673df0274",
                        "entry_id": "cea688ed-ead9-3dc5-b821-c39f97a10da7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Originally, a leather flask or vessel for oils or liquids; afterward, an earthenware vase or pitcher without a spout."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b67eaf76-fbcb-400e-bca5-470873095fa8",
                                "sense_id": "6f3b951e-0169-3e93-915b-c90673df0274",
                                "text": "Altogether, Amyx attributed fifty-six olpai and oinochoai to the Painter of Vatican 73, thus making his oeuvre rank among the largest recognized in Corinthian ceramics."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b364c24c-5e4a-47d8-9deb-a5c74f759c22",
                                "sense_id": "6f3b951e-0169-3e93-915b-c90673df0274",
                                "text": "In later vase-iconography, oinokhoai and olpai appear in sympotic scenes where they are used for ladling wine from the krater and pouring it into cups (Richter & Milne, 1935: 18; Gericke, 1970: 32-35)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8d2878e2-6a28-4f06-80ea-735a743ac1fd",
                                "sense_id": "6f3b951e-0169-3e93-915b-c90673df0274",
                                "text": "Some inscribed olpai from Athens were probably used as measures, but there are other possible domestic uses, such as for serving small amounts of oil or vinegar."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
